Fast, queryable disk-usage analyzer: a parallel scanner, queries over
its result, and a treemap in the terminal.

Without a verb, duscope scans the given path, or the current directory.

Usage: duscope [OPTIONS] [PATH]
       duscope <VERB>

Verbs:
  scan         Scan a tree and show where its space goes
  top          The largest directories or files under a path
  tree         A depth-limited tree with sizes and percentages
  by-type      Bytes and counts by file class, with the largest examples
  stale        Large files not modified (or accessed) since a date
  caches       Regenerable caches (node_modules, target, .venv, …) and their projects
  summary      A scan's totals: per volume, as du counts them, and what it left unfound
  diff         What changed between two saved scans of one tree
  explain      Facts about one path: links, clones, what deleting it frees, caches, git
  query        One read-only SQL statement over a saved scan's tables
  scans        List, prune or check the saved scans
  man          Print the manual page, in roff, to stdout
  completions  Print the completion script for a shell to stdout
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Scan:
      --threads <N>
          Worker threads (default: the platform's, from its thread sweep)
          
          [env: DUSCOPE_THREADS=]

  -x, --one-file-system
          Stay on the root's filesystem

      --include-network
          Enter network filesystems (NFS, SMB, FUSE, …)

      --follow-symlinks
          Follow symbolic links

      --size <KIND>
          The size shown: bytes on disk or apparent length

          Possible values:
          - allocated: Bytes allocated on disk
          - apparent:  Apparent length

      --apparent-size
          Show apparent sizes (--size apparent)

      --attrs <SET>
          The attributes to collect: full adds atime, btime, mode, uid and gid

          Possible values:
          - basic: Sizes, times, identity
          - full:  Basic plus atime, btime, mode, uid and gid
          
          [default: basic]

      --clones
          Count APFS clones once (on by default)

      --no-clones
          Count every clone in full

      --exclude-from <FILE>
          Don't enter directories matching these patterns (read from FILE)

      --ignore-from <FILE>
          Don't enter what these gitignore files ignore

      --exclude-caches
          Don't enter cache directories (node_modules, target, .venv, …)

      --exclude <GLOB>
          Don't enter directories matching this glob

  [PATH]
          The tree to read (default: the current directory)

Output:
      --depth <N>
          How deep the treemap is drawn (the scan reads the whole tree)
          
          [alias: --max-depth]

      --treemap <WHEN>
          When to draw the treemap

          Possible values:
          - auto:   On a terminal
          - never:  Never
          - always: Always, 80x24 when not on a terminal
          - pixels: In pixels, where the terminal shows images

      --color-by <BY>
          What colours the treemap's tiles
          
          lscolors takes LS_COLORS' *suffix= and ex= (with --attrs full) entries

          Possible values:
          - type:     File class
          - folder:   Top-level folder
          - level:    Depth
          - age:      Age
          - lscolors: LS_COLORS, else file class

      --theme <THEME>
          The colours: a theme name or the path of a theme file
          
          mocha|tokyo-night|dracula|gruvbox|light|terminal-native|no-color
          
          [env: DUSCOPE_THEME=]

      --pool-files
          Pool files into their directory's tile

      --min-tile <UNITS>
          The smallest tile the treemap draws, in half-cell units

          Possible values:
          - 1: One unit: every tile that rounds to a unit (the default)
          - 2: Two units
          - 4: Four units: a calmer picture

      --save
          Save the scan as an index and print its id

      --json
          Print the JSON envelope (--format json)

      --plain
          One tab-separated record per line, sizes in bytes (--format plain)

      --format <FORMAT>
          The output format

          Possible values:
          - treemap: The treemap (scan), on a terminal
          - table:   Tables for people
          - plain:   Tab-separated records
          - json:    The JSON envelope
          - ndjson:  One JSON object per line, every row

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour

          Possible values:
          - auto:   On a terminal, unless NO_COLOR or TERM=dumb
          - always: Always
          - never:  Never

      --no-color
          Never use colour (--color never)

      --si
          Show sizes in powers of 1,000 (kB, MB, GB)

  -b, --bytes
          Show sizes as exact byte counts

  -0, --null
          End each --plain record with NUL instead of a newline

      --header
          Start --plain output with a line of field names

      --fields <FIELDS>
          The fields of each row, comma-separated, in that order (help lists them)

      --detail
          Every field of each row

  -o, --output <FILE>
          Write the output to FILE, with no --limit cap and no --max-chars

      --max-chars <N>
          Put a JSON envelope over N characters in a file; print its first rows
          
          Kept a week in the index directory's out/; default 24000, 0 never

      --config <FILE>
          The configuration file, or none for the built-in defaults
          
          Default: DUSCOPE_CONFIG, else $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/duscope/config.toml

  -v, --verbose...
          Print phase timings to stderr; -vv adds each volume and the workers

      --debug
          Print each directory's errors to stderr as they happen

  -q, --quiet
          Don't print warnings to stderr

      --index-dir <DIR>
          Where saved scans are kept
          
          Default: $XDG_CACHE_HOME/duscope, else in the platform's cache directory
          
          [env: DUSCOPE_INDEX_DIR]
